WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:03.669
Have you ever heard about a company called
Oracle?

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:08.210
Within tech circles, Oracle is an industry
classic having been in the business since

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:09.290
1977.

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:14.809
Yet, for the average person, Oracle is a completely
unfamiliar name.

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:20.109
That’s not too surprising though given that
Oracle only has a total 430,000 customers

00:00:20.109 --> 00:00:21.109
worldwide.

00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:27.410
What’s insane though is that Oracle is valued
at $240 billion, meaning that each Oracle

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:31.840
customer translates to over $550,000 worth
of market cap.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.149
To put that into perspective, Facebook has
a total of 2.89 billion active users, and

00:00:37.149 --> 00:00:40.149
they’re valued right at $1 trillion.

00:00:40.149 --> 00:00:45.350
This means that each Facebook customer only
translates to $346 worth of market cap or

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.399
nearly 1600 times less.

00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:52.790
So, here’s how Larry Ellison built his $113
billion fortune with less than half a million

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:54.840
users worldwide.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:00.880
Taking a look back, Larry Ellison was born
on August 17, 1944 in New York City to a single

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:01.880
mother.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:06.370
His biological father was a US Army Air Corps
pilot, but it doesn’t really seem like Larry

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:08.640
had much of a relationship with him.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.380
In fact, he didn’t have much of a relationship
with his biological mother either.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:17.320
When Larry was 9 months old, he caught pneumonia,
and his mother would give Larry to her aunt

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:18.930
and uncle to raise.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:22.640
These two would basically become Larry’s
parents as Larry wouldn’t even see his biological

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:25.610
mother again until he was 48 years old.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:29.610
The uncle would give Larry the last name Ellison
as he thought this was a great way to honor

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:32.540
his entry into the US through Ellis island.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:36.330
As a kid Larry was a pretty bright kid, but
it wasn’t like he was programming at the

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:37.990
age of 12 or anything.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:41.820
He would end up attending the University of
Illinois as a premed student.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.070
After the first year, he was actually named
the science student of the year, but he would

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.830
never end up finishing his studies.

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:52.860
You see, at the end of his second year at
college, his adoptive mother would pass away

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.750
and Larry would miss his final exams.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:59.540
I’m sure he could have arranged to take
the exams at a different time given his circumstances,

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:02.240
but he would decide to just drop out altogether.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.790
Soon after, he would enroll in the University
of Chicago, but Larry would drop out of here

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:09.060
as well after the first semester.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.799
Unlike other tech billionaires, he didn’t
instantly set out on building a tech empire.

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:15.810
In fact, he was pretty lost throughout his
20s.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:20.370
He did hold several respectable jobs throughout
this time period like a job at Wells Fargo;

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:25.040
however, he was constantly switching around
and didn’t really have any purpose.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:29.120
He was able to pick up some computer programming
skills throughout this time period though,

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.970
and this would prove extremely valuable in
the 1970s.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:37.999
In 1973, Larry was working at an electronics
company called Ampex, and this is where he

00:02:37.999 --> 00:02:41.239
would meet two men named Ed Oates and Bob
Miner.

00:02:41.239 --> 00:02:46.440
By 1976 though, Larry would leave Ampex as
well and join Precision Instruments as vice

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:47.499
president.

00:02:47.499 --> 00:02:50.820
But this is when Larry would come across a
research paper written by a British computer

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.370
scientist named Edgar F. Codd.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:57.299
In the paper, Codd described how large amounts
of data could be efficiently stored using

00:02:57.299 --> 00:02:59.260
a relational database model.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:03.230
Larry very quickly realized the commercial
potential of such a product and would reach

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:07.209
out to his former colleagues Ed Oates and
Bob Miner about the idea.

00:03:07.209 --> 00:03:11.069
The three would get together and start developing
a database solution based on Codd’s research

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:12.230
paper.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:16.739
It seems like Larry was the most convinced
about the idea though as he fronted $1200

00:03:16.739 --> 00:03:18.909
of the total $2000 they started with.

00:03:18.909 --> 00:03:23.689
Originally, they named the company Software
Development Laboratories, but by the time

00:03:23.689 --> 00:03:28.749
they finished development in 1979, they would
change the name to Relational Software Inc.

00:03:28.749 --> 00:03:34.500
In 1979, the trio would launch their database
product which they called Oracle to the public.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:39.530
Oracle was the first commercial SQL based
relational database program on the market.

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:45.209
But, 1979 was simply way too early for such
a product, and no one really cared.

00:03:45.209 --> 00:03:49.959
So, the team had to be extremely aggressive
with their marketing effort and even be a

00:03:49.959 --> 00:03:50.959
little sleazy.

00:03:50.959 --> 00:03:53.260
One example of this was the naming of the
product itself.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:58.690
You see, the product wasn’t just called
Oracle, it was actually called Oracle V2 despite

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:00.370
it being the first version.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:03.980
Larry Ellison figured that people would be
much more likely to buy a second generation

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:07.700
product, so he just went ahead and called
the product V2.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:11.849
Despite this though, it was still extremely
difficult to make sales because once again,

00:04:11.849 --> 00:04:13.769
no one cared at the time.

00:04:13.769 --> 00:04:17.700
Fortunately for the team though, the CIA would
give them a shot and become the company’s

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.380
first major customer.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:23.970
Given that the company was only known for
Oracle V2, in 1982, the team would decide

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:27.500
to change the name of the company to Oracle
Systems Corporation.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:31.230
Over the next couple of years, they would
launch newer versions of Oracle, and as you

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:34.030
would guess each version was 1 off.

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:39.160
Version 2 was called Oracle V3, version 3
was cooled Oracle V4, and so on.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:44.290
By 1986, Oracle started to gain some serious
traction and they would decide to go public.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:50.180
At IPO, each share of Oracle was worth $15
which gave the company a valuation of $194

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:51.180
million.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:56.030
So, not quite a billion dollar company, but
still massive nonetheless.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:05:01.510
In 1987, Oracle would become the largest database
management company in the world, but this

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:05.530
would unfortunately be the company’s last
highlight for quite some time.

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:10.220
In 1990, an internal audit would reveal that
Oracle had been overstating their earnings.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:14.190
It seems like the sales team was counting
orders as sales and this inflated Oracle’s

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:16.180
top line for a few quarters.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:20.950
Oracle would come out with revised quarterly
reports, and they weren’t half bad, but

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:23.950
investors were not happy nonetheless.

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:28.110
Looking back, you can’t even see this on
Oracle’s stock graph as it was so insignificant

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:31.280
in comparison to Oracle’s stock price today.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:36.200
But at the time, this scandal led to a 75%
decline in Oracle’s stock price.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:40.660
Oracle also started to face increasing competition
within the database market as more companies

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:42.210
entered the scene.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:46.401
As a result, in an effort to diversify and
continue growing, Larry would attempt to take

00:05:46.401 --> 00:05:49.010
Oracle into the personal computer market.

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:53.040
He actually had a brilliant idea to reduce
the cost of personal computers by leveraging

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.300
databases and networks.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:59.650
His idea was to create something called the
NC or the network computer.

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:04.210
This computer would basically just be a barebones
screen and input device while the actual computing

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:07.370
and processing would be completed on Oracle’s
networks.

00:06:07.370 --> 00:06:11.450
He basically wanted to use cloud computing
to reduce the cost of computers.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:15.400
But as you might’ve guessed, the technology
just wasn’t there to create such a product

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:16.710
in the 1990s.

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:21.830
Moreover, the cost of personal computers were
plummeting as technology continued to advance,

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:24.720
so it made less and less sense to create the
NC.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.590
By the end of the 1990s, Oracle had made little
progress.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:33.740
They were still the largest database company
in the world, but the NC didn’t go anywhere

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:37.360
and Oracle now had a major competitor called
Informix.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:41.570
Around this time though, internet companies
were blowing up as the dotcom bubble picked

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:42.580
up steam.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:46.720
Larry Ellison would clevely pick up on this
opportunity and embrace the internet.

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.070
He would offer Oracle’s various database
and network products through the internet.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:53.500
In the meantime, Informix would fizzle out
by themselves.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:59.130
In 1997, Informix would have their own earnings
scandal and the CEO, Phil White, would end

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:00.230
up in jail.

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:05.400
So, Oracle’s major problems would unexpectedly
be solved overnight and this allowed Oracle

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:09.140
to rally exponentially along with the rest
of the dotcom companies.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:14.720
Between 1999 and 2000, Oracle stock would
grow from just $6 to $40.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:19.120
But by 2002, they would crash back down to
$10 per share.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:22.960
Despite their share price crashing, Oracle
was in a better position than ever.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:28.410
They had little major competition and they
had a new growth venue which was the internet.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:32.890
Given their strong position in the market,
Oracle would go on a shopping spree, and they’ve

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:35.380
been on a shopping spree ever since.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:39.190
If you look through their list of acquisitions,
you’ll see that they made 55 acquisitions

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:42.040
between 2000 and 2010 alone.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:47.820
One of their most famous acquisitions of all
time was Sun Microsystems for $7.4 billion

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:52.150
which gave Oracle control over the Java programming
platform.

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:55.820
Throughout the 2010s, Oracle has basically
just stuck to doing the same thing.

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:59.840
They still provide database solutions and
acquire a bunch of companies, but they have

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:04.200
expanded to cloud services as well which has
proved to be quite lucrative.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:09.300
But that still leaves the question, how did
Oracle make so much money with such few customers?

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:14.640
Well, the answer is pretty simple, their entire
customer base is enterprises who pay Oracle

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:17.410
massive amounts of money every single year.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:21.700
And when I say massive, I don’t mean thousands
or even tens of thousands per year.

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:25.140
I’m talking hundreds of thousands and millions
per year.

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:29.040
When we take a look at the size of their customers,
this isn’t that surprising.

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:33.979
For Oracle’s Enterprise Resource Planning
cloud or Oracle ERP, 48% of their customers

00:08:33.979 --> 00:08:39.669
make less than $50 million per year, 10% make
between $50 million and $1 billion per year,

00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:42.949
and 30% make over $1 billion per year.

00:08:42.949 --> 00:08:47.649
Basically every single company and organization
you can think of uses Oracle or has used them

00:08:47.649 --> 00:08:48.930
in the past.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:55.240
Everyone from Netflix, Google, Amazon, Walmart,
Chase, MIT, governments, JetBlue, Dell, eBay,

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:57.580
you get the point.

00:08:57.580 --> 00:08:58.750
Basically everyone.

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:02.980
Considering this, it’s not surprising that
Oracle is worth $245 billion today.

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:07.589
In fact, I’m surprised that it’s not worth
more given the massive valuations of other

00:09:07.589 --> 00:09:08.980
tech companies.

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:14.670
Right now, Oracle only has a PE ratio of 19.31
which is the lowest I’ve seen in the tech

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:16.449
industry recently.

00:09:16.449 --> 00:09:20.800
But anyway, going back to Larry Ellison, he
stepped down from being Oracle’s CEO in

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:26.959
2014 after 37 years, but he still owns 35%
of the company today.

00:09:26.959 --> 00:09:29.779
This translates to about $85 billion.

00:09:29.779 --> 00:09:35.540
But Larry is actually worth $113 billion today
due to a variety of other investments.

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:39.230
He has over a billion in real estate and he
used to have a multi billion dollar position

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:40.430
in Netsuite.

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:44.550
Aside from this, it’s also believed that
he has a large position in SalesForce, but

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.519
this has not been confirmed.

00:09:46.519 --> 00:09:51.600
Larry also owns Oracle team USA which is a
yacht racing team who have won several America’s

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:52.600
cups.

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:57.610
However, by far his most famous investment
was in December of 2018, when he bought 3

00:09:57.610 --> 00:09:59.339
million shares of Tesla.

00:09:59.339 --> 00:10:04.230
Adjusting for the stock split, that’s the
same as 15 million shares of Tesla today.

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:08.010
Right after he bought it, he actually got
screwed as Tesla crashed 50 percent in early

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:09.519
2019.

00:10:09.519 --> 00:10:13.910
But as we all know, Tesla has since recovered
and soared to unbelievable heights.

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:18.759
Larry’s stake in Tesla is now worth over
$10 billion and he’s actually a Tesla board

00:10:18.759 --> 00:10:20.749
member today.

00:10:20.749 --> 00:10:25.959
And that’s the story of how Larry Ellison
made $113 billion with less than half a million

00:10:25.959 --> 00:10:26.959
customers.

00:10:26.959 --> 00:10:28.550
Did you guys know about Larry Ellison?

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:29.860
